[0.699, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\r\u001b[27A\u001b[K  ➡️ \u001b[1mYes\u001b[m: \u001b[94m(Container, Slot)\u001b[m is strictly unique (1 Part per Slot; the picker shows which slots are already taken and\u001b[?7h\u001b[116G\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  warns/prevents double-assigning), while Parts without a Slot (either unassigned to any Container, or placed in a\u001b[?7h\u001b[115G\r\n  Container with no Slot specified) don't conflict with each other.\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\n\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[m\r\n\u001b[34;1m> yes\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\u001b[?7l\n\u001b[K  ❓ \u001b[1mQ5\u001b[m - \u001b[1mContainer Lifecycle (First-Class Entity vs. String Tag)\u001b[m: Should a \u001b[1mContainer\u001b[m be a managed entity in the JSON\u001b[?7h\u001b[118G\u001b[?7l\r\n\u001b[K  store (with an \u001b[94mid\u001b[m, \u001b[94mname\u001b[m, and optional \u001b[94mdescription\u001b[m, so you can rename \u001b[94m\"Drawer Cabinet 1\"\u001b[m in one place and browse\u001b[?7h\u001b[114G\r\n  empty containers), while a \u001b[1mSlot\u001b[m is a simple string label (e.g., \u001b[94m\"A1\"\u001b[m) on the Part withi"]
